{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And the tablets were the work of Elohim.</b> The intention is not artificial craftsmanship but the work of Elohim through His word, as it says (Psalms 33:6): ״בדבר ה' שמים נעשו״, and it is written (Genesis 2:4): ״ביום עשות ה' אלהים ארץ ושמיםם״." "<b>And the writing was the writing of Elohim.</b> The Ramban wrote that the verse that comes in praise of the tablets should properly have appeared earlier, near the verse 'and He gave to Moses'; but the matter is that when Moses came here to break the tablets, he wished to mention the verse of their praise and excellence, to make known that despite all this Moses did not refrain from breaking them when he saw that evil deed — thus the Rav, of blessed memory, explained it. Nevertheless one may wonder about Moses, the servant of HaShem, faithful in His house: how did his heart presume to break the tablets that were the writing of Elohim? And if Israel sinned and were not worthy of the Torah, he should have returned the Torah to its repository and asked the Holy One, blessed be He, what to do with it — if a king of flesh and blood sends his sealed letter by the hand of his servant, faithful in his house, to the ministers of the kingdom, and they refuse to accept it, the faithful servant ought to return it to the king, not treat it with contempt and tear it up! But the breaking of the tablets by our master Moses was because he saw that the writing flew off the tablets when they drew near the boundary of the calf, in the place of impurity and sin. And all the honor that people show for the king's writing is only because of the seal; once the writing flew off here, the seal of the King departed." "<b>And know</b> that the writing on the tablets was like the soul in the body, and when the writing departed the tablets remained a body without a soul — and a body without a soul is fit to be buried under the ground. Therefore his judgment agreed to break them beneath the mountain. And a proof that the writing flew off the tablets is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'Take heed with an elder who has forgotten his learning due to circumstance beyond his control' — as Rabbi Ami said: 'The tablets and the fragments of the tablets lie in the Ark,' that is, the fragments of tablets from which the writing had flown. Therefore he brings a proof from an elder from whom the Torah has flown and who has become merely the receptacle of the Torah. And this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: that the tablets fell from his hand on account of their heaviness, for they had become heavy — since at first it is written: ״ושני לוחות העדות בידו״ — 'in his hand,' meaning in one hand; and when he saw the calf and the dances, it says: ״וישלך מידיו את הלוחות״ — that even with both hands he could not carry them. And so it is with a person: as long as the soul is within him and he is alive, his weight is not as great as it is after death, when the soul has departed from him. And likewise a piece of iron: while it is in the fire and glowing, it is lighter because of the fire, which is the soul within it and bears it up — as the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'the living bears itself' — but afterward, when it cools and the fire departs from it with the departure of its soul, for it has died, then it is as heavy as it was at first."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:27.451018005Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}